==German==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|houwen}}, {{inh|de|goh|houwan}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*hauwan}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*hawwaną}}. Cognate with {{cog|en|hew}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|[ˈhaʊ̯ən]|[ˈhaʊ̯n]}}
* {{audio|de|De-hauen.ogg|Audio}}
* {{hyphenation|de|hau|en}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb|hauen<haute:hieb,gehauen:gehaut[only in some regional vernaculars]>}}

# {{lb|de|literary}} to [[thrust]], [[slash]] (with a weapon)
#: {{uxi|de|Er '''hieb''' nach meinem Helm.|He thrust for my helmet.}}
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[cut]], [[hew]]
#: {{uxi|de|Der Einbrecher '''haut''' ein Loch in die Wand.|The burglar cuts a hole in the wall.}}
# {{lb|de|colloquial}} to [[hit]], [[beat]], [[bang]], [[whack]]
#: {{uxi|de|Meine Mutter hat ihn '''gehauen'''.|My mother hit him.}}
#: {{uxi|de|Sie '''haute''' die Nägel in die Wand.|She banged the nails into the wall}}
# {{lb|de|reflexive|colloquial}} to [[fling]] oneself
#: {{uxi|de|Ich werde mich in die Falle '''hauen'''.|I'm going to hit the sack}}
# {{lb|de|regional}} to [[chop]], [[chop down]]

====Usage notes====
The strong preterite form {{m|de|hieb}} is only common in the literary sense of ''to [[thrust]]'', and also in the set phrase ''[[in die gleiche Kerbe hauen]]'', otherwise it's rare and/or stilted, with {{m|de|haute}} being used instead. However, the main past participle is still {{m|de|gehauen}} outside of some regional vernaculars.

====Conjugation====
{{de-conj|hauen<haute:hieb,gehauen:gehaut[only in some regional vernaculars]>}}

